{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001267","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 9 candidates are direct matches to ankylosing spondylitis by trait_efo and phenotyping_reported. However, the pool is dominated by framework/benchmarking-style UK Biobank-derived scores (PGS001267/PGS001268 from the “813 traits” sparse framework; PGS001876/PGS002089 from the “Portability of 245 polygenic scores” framework) and a small Taiwanese EAS-only study family (PGS003420–PGS003424) with unknown covariates and only full-model AUROC reported, limiting cross-model comparability.

PGS001267 is preferred because it reports PRS-comparable metrics explicitly (PGS AUROC (no covariates)=0.7265; PGS R² (no covariates)=0.0988) plus full-model AUROC with only basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs), and its PRS-only R² is below the provided heritability ceiling (h2_liability≈0.399), passing the sanity check. Competing framework candidates from the portability paper lack PRS-comparable AUC/R² (only partial-r), and the EAS-only Taiwanese models report only full-model AUROC with unknown covariates and much smaller validation (n=1,298), making their apparent AUROC advantage non-comparable.

Main limitation: PGS001267 is from a pan-trait sparse UKB framework (a known weaker archetype for external generalization per the provided domain knowledge), and the time-to-event sibling (PGS001268) has slightly higher full-model AUROC; without external disease-focused multi-cohort candidates or consistent PRS-only metrics across studies, the recommendation remains sub-optimal rather than high-confidence."}

