{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait “nodular goiter”, including (i) nontoxic multinodular goiter scores (PGS001814, PGS002022) and (ii) benign nodular goiter scores (PGS005261, PGS005262, PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273, PGS005274) are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter and are less endpoint-faithful for a generic nodular goiter target.

Among the most phenotype-aligned options, PGS005262 is preferred because it is explicitly for “Benign nodular goiter” (closest lexical/clinical match to nodular goiter) and, within its coherent disease-focused study family, it has the strongest reported discrimination evidence available in the context (highest full-model AUROC among the benign nodular goiter models: 0.5933 vs 0.5854 and 0.5876) and the largest per-SD effect size (OR 1.408 vs 1.362 and 1.048). Methodologically it is also a genome-wide continuous-shrinkage approach (PRSCS) with ~1.09M variants, which is structurally favorable versus the sparse P+T alternative.

Limitations lowering confidence: the reported AUROC appears to be full-model (not explicitly PGS-only), covariates are listed as “Unknown” for the 2026 medRxiv models, and the alternative UKB portability-framework multinodular goiter models (PGS001814/PGS002022) have explicit PRS-only-style metrics (partial-r) but are from a portability framework and show similarly small effects (partial-r ~0.024–0.028). With no clearly comparable PGS-only AUC/R2 across all candidates, the selection remains sub-optimal rather than high-confidence."}

