{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002768","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 13 candidates are direct matches to the target trait (osteoporosis) by trait_reported/trait_efo and generally coherent phenotyping_reported. Several candidates come from pan-trait or portability framework publications (e.g., PGS001273/PGS001274 from the 'across 813 traits' UKB framework; PGS001883/PGS002095 from the 'Portability of 245 polygenic scores' framework), which domain_knowledge flags as typically underperforming disease-focused alternatives despite having cleaner-looking catalog metrics. Many newer candidates (PGS004490/PGS004495/PGS004560/PGS004565; PGS004809/PGS004810; PGS005146/PGS005155) lack PRS-comparable AUC/R2 and often report only a single per-SD effect size or incremental R2, with some having unknown covariates, limiting comparability.

PGS002768 is selected because it has strong endpoint alignment (phenotyping_reported: Osteoporosis; trait_efo includes osteoporosis) and provides a moderate, interpretable PRS effect size (OR=1.31 per SD with CI 1.25–1.38) under basic covariates (age, sex, PCs, technical covariates) in a reasonably sized European evaluation (n=39,444). This offers more directly PRS-relevant evidence than candidates with missing metrics and unknown covariates, while avoiding the framework-penalized UKB pan-trait/portability scores.

Main limitation: evidence remains incomplete/heterogeneous across the pool—several candidates lack comparable PRS-only metrics, and PGS002768 does not report AUROC/R2 here—so the choice is not unequivocally best-supported, keeping confidence at Moderate and the outcome at DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL."}

